WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:23.200
I have spent the past few years putting myself into situations that are usually very difficult

00:23.200 --> 00:25.800
and at the same time somewhat dangerous.

00:25.800 --> 00:30.880
I went to prison, difficult.

00:30.880 --> 00:35.640
I worked in a coal mine, dangerous.

00:35.640 --> 00:40.200
I filmed in war zones, difficult and dangerous.

00:40.200 --> 00:46.360
And I spent 30 days eating nothing but this, fun in the beginning, little difficult in

00:46.360 --> 00:49.040
the middle, very dangerous in the end.

00:49.040 --> 00:56.880
In fact, most of my career I have been immersing myself into seemingly horrible situations

00:56.880 --> 01:02.240
for the whole goal of trying to examine societal issues in a way that make them engaging, that

01:02.240 --> 01:06.540
make them interesting, that hopefully break them down in a way that make them entertaining

01:06.540 --> 01:08.740
and accessible to an audience.

01:08.740 --> 01:12.160
So when I knew I was coming here to do a TED Talk that was going to look at the world of

01:12.160 --> 01:16.200
branding and sponsorship, I knew I would want to do something a little different.

01:16.200 --> 01:22.040
So as some of you may or may not have heard, a couple weeks ago I took out an ad on eBay.

01:22.040 --> 01:27.920
I sent out some Facebook messages, some Twitter messages, and I gave people the opportunity

01:27.920 --> 01:33.520
to buy the naming rights to my 2011 TED Talk.

01:33.520 --> 01:38.320
That's right, some lucky individual, corporation, for-profit or non-profit, was going to get

01:38.320 --> 01:41.320
the once-in-a-lifetime opportunity, because I'm sure Chris Anderson will never let it

01:41.320 --> 01:47.920
happen again, to buy the naming rights to the talk you're watching right now, that at

01:47.920 --> 01:52.040
the time didn't have a title, didn't really have a lot of content, and didn't really give

01:52.040 --> 01:55.760
much hint as to what the subject matter would actually be.

01:55.760 --> 01:58.000
So what you were getting was this.

01:58.000 --> 02:03.200
Your name here presents my TED Talk, that you have no idea what the subject is and depending

02:03.200 --> 02:07.720
on the content could ultimately blow up in your face, especially if I make you or your

02:07.720 --> 02:09.320
company look stupid for doing it.

02:09.320 --> 02:19.160
But that being said, it's a very good media opportunity.

02:19.160 --> 02:25.040
You know how many people watch these TED Talks, it's a lot.

02:25.040 --> 02:28.680
That's just a working title by the way.

02:28.680 --> 02:34.080
So even with that caveat, I knew that someone would buy the naming rights.

02:34.080 --> 02:36.400
Now if you'd have asked me that a year ago, I wouldn't have been able to tell you that

02:36.400 --> 02:40.240
with any certainty, but in the new project that I'm working on, my new film, we examine

02:40.240 --> 02:45.280
the world of marketing, advertising, and as I said earlier, I put myself in some pretty

02:45.280 --> 02:51.120
horrible situations over the years, but nothing could prepare me, nothing could ready me for

02:51.120 --> 03:03.120
anything as difficult or as dangerous as going into the rooms with these guys.

03:03.120 --> 03:05.760
You see, I had this idea for a movie.

03:05.760 --> 03:10.680
What I want to do is make a film all about product placement, marketing, and advertising,

03:10.680 --> 03:14.800
where the entire film is funded by product placement, marketing, and advertising.

03:14.800 --> 03:17.720
So the movie will be called The Greatest Movie Ever Sold.

03:17.720 --> 03:21.480
So what happens in The Greatest Movie Ever Sold is that everything from top to bottom,

03:21.480 --> 03:24.880
from start to finish, is branded from beginning to end.

03:24.880 --> 03:28.400
From the above the title sponsor that you'll see in the movie, which is Brand X.

03:28.400 --> 03:33.760
Now this brand, the Qualcomm Stadium, the Staples Center, these people will be married

03:33.760 --> 03:37.840
to the film In Perpetuity Forever, and so the film explores this whole idea.

03:37.840 --> 03:38.840
It's what?

03:38.840 --> 03:39.840
In Perpetuity Forever.

03:39.840 --> 03:40.840
I'm a redundant person.

03:40.840 --> 03:41.840
I'm just saying.

03:41.840 --> 03:47.440
That was more in perpetuity, period, forever, but not only are we going to have the Brand

03:47.440 --> 03:51.320
X title sponsor, but we're going to make sure we sell out every category we can in the film.

03:51.320 --> 03:54.200
So maybe we sell a shoe and it becomes the greatest shoe you ever wore, the greatest

03:54.200 --> 03:57.940
car you ever drove from The Greatest Movie Ever Sold, you know, the greatest drink you've

03:57.940 --> 03:59.920
ever had, courtesy of The Greatest Movie Ever Sold.

03:59.960 --> 04:04.480
So the idea is, beyond just showing that brands are part of your life, but actually get them

04:04.480 --> 04:05.480
to finance the film?

04:05.480 --> 04:08.760
Get them to finance the film, and actually we show the whole process of how does it work.

04:08.760 --> 04:10.960
The goal of this whole film is transparency.

04:10.960 --> 04:13.280
You're going to see the whole thing take place in this movie.

04:13.280 --> 04:17.960
So that's the whole concept, the whole film, start to finish, and I would love for CEG

04:17.960 --> 04:18.960
to help make it happen.

04:18.960 --> 04:25.860
Yeah, you know, it's funny because when I first hear it, it is the ultimate respect

04:25.860 --> 04:26.860
for an audience.

04:26.900 --> 04:30.740
I don't know how receptive people are going to be to it, though.

04:30.740 --> 04:33.980
Do you have a perspective, or do you, I don't want to use angle because that sort of has

04:33.980 --> 04:37.420
a negative connotation, but do you sort of know how this is going to play out?

04:37.420 --> 04:38.420
No idea.

04:38.420 --> 04:41.420
How much money does it take to do this?

04:41.420 --> 04:42.420
1.5 million.

04:42.420 --> 04:43.420
Good, good.

04:43.420 --> 04:47.460
I think that you're going to have a hard time meeting with them, but I think it's certainly

04:47.460 --> 04:51.220
worth pursuing a couple of really big, obvious brands.

04:51.220 --> 04:52.220
Who knows?

04:52.220 --> 04:55.020
Maybe by the time your film comes out, we look like a bunch of blithering idiots.

04:55.020 --> 04:56.620
What do you think the response is going to be?

04:57.380 --> 04:59.740
The response is mostly will be no.

04:59.740 --> 05:03.260
But is the toughest sell because of the film, or is the toughest sell because of me?

05:03.260 --> 05:04.260
Both.

05:04.260 --> 05:07.380
That meaning, not so optimistic.

05:07.380 --> 05:10.660
So, sir, can you help me?

05:10.660 --> 05:11.660
I need help.

05:11.660 --> 05:12.660
I can help.

05:12.660 --> 05:13.660
Okay.

05:13.660 --> 05:14.660
Good.

05:14.660 --> 05:15.660
Good.

05:15.660 --> 05:16.660
Awesome.

05:16.660 --> 05:18.980
We've got to figure out which brands.

05:18.980 --> 05:19.980
Yeah.

05:19.980 --> 05:22.120
That's the challenge.

05:22.120 --> 05:24.060
When you look at the people you deal with.

05:24.060 --> 05:25.380
We've got some places we can go.

05:25.380 --> 05:26.380
Okay.

05:27.140 --> 05:28.140
Turn the camera off.

05:28.140 --> 05:32.020
I thought turn the camera off meant let's have an off-the-record conversation.

05:32.020 --> 05:37.220
Turns out it really means we want nothing to do with your movie.

05:37.220 --> 05:43.220
And just like that, one by one, all of these companies suddenly disappeared.

05:43.220 --> 05:45.140
None of them wanted anything to do with this movie.

05:45.140 --> 05:46.500
I was amazed.

05:46.500 --> 05:48.740
They wanted absolutely nothing to do with this project.

05:48.740 --> 05:51.540
I was blown away because I thought the whole concept, the idea of advertising, was to get

05:51.540 --> 05:55.020
your product out in front of as many people as possible, to get to as many people to see

05:55.020 --> 05:56.020
it as possible.

05:56.020 --> 06:00.660
Especially in today's world, this intersection of new media and old media and the fractured

06:00.660 --> 06:06.880
media landscape isn't the idea to get that new buzzworthy delivery vehicle that's going

06:06.880 --> 06:10.100
to get that message to the masses.

06:10.100 --> 06:11.420
That's what I thought.

06:11.420 --> 06:17.700
But the problem was, you see, my idea had one fatal flaw.

06:17.700 --> 06:20.140
And that flaw was this.

06:20.140 --> 06:22.940
Actually, no, that was not the flaw whatsoever.

06:22.940 --> 06:24.500
That wouldn't have been a problem at all.

06:24.980 --> 06:26.620
See, this would have been fine.

06:26.620 --> 06:28.580
But what this image represents was the problem.

06:28.580 --> 06:39.060
See, when you do a Google image search for transparency, this is one of the first images

06:39.060 --> 06:40.220
that comes up.

06:40.220 --> 06:43.340
So I like the way you roll, Sergey Brin.

06:43.340 --> 06:44.340
No.

06:48.340 --> 06:51.300
This, this was the problem, transparency.

06:51.300 --> 06:57.660
Free from pretense or deceit, easily detected or seen through, readily understood, characterized

06:57.660 --> 07:02.340
by visibility or accessibility of information, especially concerning business practices.

07:02.340 --> 07:05.200
That last line being probably the biggest problem.

07:05.200 --> 07:07.380
You see, we hear a lot about transparency these days.

07:07.380 --> 07:12.580
Our politicians say it, our president says it, even our CEOs say it.

07:12.580 --> 07:16.060
But suddenly when it comes down to it becoming a reality, something suddenly changes.

07:16.060 --> 07:17.060
But why?

07:17.060 --> 07:21.420
Well, transparency is scary.

07:21.420 --> 07:26.200
Like that odd still screaming bear.

07:26.200 --> 07:32.380
It's unpredictable.

07:32.380 --> 07:35.740
Like this odd country road.

07:35.740 --> 07:38.980
And it's also very risky.

07:38.980 --> 07:43.220
What else is?

07:43.220 --> 07:44.220
What else is risky?

07:44.380 --> 07:52.500
Eating an entire bowl of Cool Whip.

07:52.500 --> 07:54.140
That's very risky.

07:54.140 --> 07:58.820
No, when I started talking to companies, you know, and telling them, you know, we wanted

07:58.820 --> 08:02.500
to tell this story and they said, no, we want you to tell a story.

08:02.500 --> 08:03.500
We want you to tell a story.

08:03.500 --> 08:06.380
But we just want to tell our story.

08:06.380 --> 08:11.460
See, when I was a kid and my father would catch me in some sort of a lie, there he is

08:11.460 --> 08:18.380
giving me the look he often gave me, he would say, son, there's three sides to every story.

08:18.380 --> 08:20.780
There's your story.

08:20.780 --> 08:22.740
There's my story.

08:22.740 --> 08:23.980
And there's the real story.

08:23.980 --> 08:27.580
Now, you see, with this film, we wanted to tell the real story.

08:27.580 --> 08:30.900
But with only one company, one agency willing to help me, and that's only because I knew

08:30.900 --> 08:36.060
John Bond and Richard Kirshenbaum for years, I realized that I would have to go on my own.

08:36.060 --> 08:40.260
I'd have to cut out the middleman and go to the companies myself, you know, myself with

08:40.260 --> 08:41.260
all of my team.

08:41.260 --> 08:44.620
So, what you suddenly started to realize, or what I started to realize, is that when

08:44.620 --> 08:47.180
you started having conversations with these companies, the idea of understanding your

08:47.180 --> 08:49.700
brand is a universal problem.

08:49.700 --> 08:53.020
I have friends who make great big giant Hollywood films, and I have friends who make little

08:53.020 --> 08:55.140
independent films like I make.

08:55.140 --> 08:57.940
And the friends of mine who make big giant Hollywood movies say that the reason their

08:57.940 --> 09:01.420
films are so successful is because of the brand partners that they have.

09:01.420 --> 09:04.900
And then my friends who make small independent films say, well, how are we supposed to compete

09:04.900 --> 09:06.580
with these big giant Hollywood movies?

09:06.580 --> 09:10.340
And the movie is called The Greatest Movie Ever Sold.

09:10.340 --> 09:14.380
So, now how specifically will we see band in the film?

09:14.380 --> 09:18.300
Anytime I'm getting ready to go, anytime I open up my medicine cabinet, you will see

09:18.300 --> 09:19.300
band deodorant.

09:19.300 --> 09:24.900
Well, anytime I do an interview with someone, I could say, are you fresh enough for this

09:24.900 --> 09:25.900
interview?

09:25.900 --> 09:26.900
Are you ready?

09:26.900 --> 09:27.900
You look a little nervous.

09:27.900 --> 09:31.140
I want to help you calm down, so maybe you should put some on for the interview.

09:31.140 --> 09:35.180
So, we'll offer one of these fabulous sets, whether it's a floral fusion or a paradise

09:35.180 --> 09:37.020
winds, you know, they'll have their chance.

09:37.020 --> 09:41.340
We will have them geared for both male or female, you know, solid roll on her stick,

09:41.340 --> 09:43.340
whatever it may be.

09:43.340 --> 09:44.740
That's the two cent tour.

09:44.740 --> 09:49.040
So, now I can answer any of your questions and give you the five cent tour.

09:49.040 --> 09:53.420
We are a smaller brand, much like you talked about being a smaller, you know, movie, we're

09:53.420 --> 09:57.020
very much a challenger brand, so we don't have the budgets that other brands have.

09:57.020 --> 10:02.260
So, doing things like this, you know, remind people about band is kind of why we're interested

10:02.260 --> 10:03.260
in it.

10:03.260 --> 10:04.860
What are the words that you would use to describe band?

10:04.860 --> 10:05.860
Band is blank.

10:05.860 --> 10:20.260
Technology is not the way you want to describe something somebody's putting in their armpit.

10:20.260 --> 10:25.740
We talk about bold, fresh, I think fresh is a great word that really spins this category

10:25.740 --> 10:29.500
into the positive versus the fights odor and wetness.

10:29.500 --> 10:30.500
Right.

10:30.500 --> 10:31.500
It keeps you fresh.

10:31.500 --> 10:34.340
How do we keep you fresher, longer, better freshness, more freshness?

10:34.340 --> 10:39.260
Three times fresher, things like that that are, you know, more of that positive benefit.

10:39.260 --> 10:42.180
And that's a multimillion dollar corporation.

10:42.180 --> 10:43.180
What about me?

10:43.180 --> 10:44.180
What about a regular guy?

10:44.180 --> 10:45.580
I need to go talk to the man on the street.

10:45.580 --> 10:49.580
The people who are like me, the regular Joes, they need to tell me about my brand.

10:49.580 --> 10:55.420
How would you guys describe your brand?

10:55.420 --> 10:56.420
My brand?

10:56.420 --> 11:01.260
I don't know, like, I like really nice clothes.

11:01.260 --> 11:06.340
80s revival meets skater punk, unless it's laundry day.

11:06.340 --> 11:07.340
Right.

11:07.340 --> 11:08.340
What is brand Jerry?

11:08.340 --> 11:09.340
Unique.

11:09.340 --> 11:10.340
Unique.

11:10.340 --> 11:16.020
I guess what kind of genre of style I am would be like dark glamour.

11:16.020 --> 11:19.700
I like a lot of black colors, a lot of grays and stuff like that.

11:19.700 --> 11:26.460
But usually I have an accessory like sunglasses or I like crystal and things like that, too.

11:26.460 --> 11:34.300
If Dan were a brand, he might be a classic, convertible Mercedes Benz.

11:34.300 --> 11:38.540
The brand that I am is, I would call it casual fly.

11:38.540 --> 11:42.340
Part hippie, part yogi, part Brooklyn girl, I don't know.

11:42.340 --> 11:43.340
I'm the pet guy.

11:43.340 --> 11:46.420
I sell pet toys all over the country, all over the world.

11:46.420 --> 11:50.300
So I guess that's my brand and my warped little industry.

11:50.300 --> 11:51.340
That's my brand.

11:51.340 --> 11:54.420
My brand is FedEx because I deliver the goods.

11:54.420 --> 11:57.180
Failed writer alcoholic brand.

11:57.180 --> 11:58.180
Is that something?

11:58.180 --> 12:04.340
I'm a lawyer brand.

12:04.340 --> 12:05.740
I'm Tom.

12:05.740 --> 12:11.020
Well, we can't all be brand Tom, but I do often find myself at the intersection of dark

12:11.020 --> 12:14.900
glamour and casual fly.

12:14.900 --> 12:16.460
What I realized, I needed an expert.

12:16.460 --> 12:20.380
I need somebody who could get inside my head, somebody who could really help me understand

12:20.380 --> 12:22.500
what they call your brand personality.

12:22.500 --> 12:25.860
And so I found a company called Olson's Altman in Pittsburgh, who they've helped companies

12:25.860 --> 12:29.700
like Nestle, Febreze, Hallmark discover that brand personality.

12:29.700 --> 12:33.180
If they could do it for them, surely they could do it for me.

12:33.180 --> 12:34.340
You brought your pictures, right?

12:34.340 --> 12:35.340
I did.

12:35.340 --> 12:38.740
The very first picture is a picture of my family.

12:38.740 --> 12:41.660
So tell me a little bit how it relates to your thoughts and feelings about who you are.

12:41.660 --> 12:44.500
These are the people who shape the way I look at the world.

12:44.500 --> 12:45.500
Tell me about this world.

12:45.500 --> 12:46.500
This world?

12:46.500 --> 12:47.500
Yeah.

12:47.500 --> 12:49.060
I think your world is the world that you live in.

12:49.060 --> 12:52.620
The people that are around you, your friends, your family, the way you live your life, the

12:52.620 --> 12:56.940
job you do, all those things stemmed and started from one place, and for me, they stemmed and

12:56.940 --> 12:59.060
started with my family in West Virginia.

12:59.060 --> 13:00.180
What's the next one you want to talk about?

13:00.180 --> 13:02.820
The next one, this is the best day ever.

13:02.820 --> 13:04.700
How does this relate to thoughts and feelings about who you are?

13:04.700 --> 13:06.580
It's like, who do I want to be?

13:06.580 --> 13:07.820
I like things that are different.

13:07.820 --> 13:09.620
I like things that are weird.

13:09.620 --> 13:10.620
I like weird things.

13:10.620 --> 13:12.140
Tell me about the Y phase.

13:12.140 --> 13:13.500
What does that do for us?

13:13.500 --> 13:14.500
What is the machete?

13:14.500 --> 13:15.500
What pupa stage are you in now?

13:15.500 --> 13:16.500
Why is it important to reboot?

13:16.500 --> 13:17.500
What does the red represent?

13:17.940 --> 13:18.940
Tell me a little bit about that heart.

13:18.940 --> 13:21.940
Tell me a little bit about you that's not who you are now.

13:21.940 --> 13:23.940
What are some other metamorphosis that you have?

13:23.940 --> 13:24.940
You have to be feared.

13:24.940 --> 13:25.940
What kind of roller coaster are you on?

13:25.940 --> 13:26.940
Thank you.

13:26.940 --> 13:27.940
Thank you.

13:27.940 --> 13:28.940
Yeah, thanks for your patience.

13:28.940 --> 13:29.940
Great job.

13:29.940 --> 13:30.940
Yeah.

13:30.940 --> 13:31.940
Thanks a lot.

13:31.940 --> 13:32.940
All right.

13:32.940 --> 13:33.940
Yeah.

13:33.940 --> 13:35.500
I don't know what's going to come of this.

13:35.500 --> 13:37.740
There was a whole lot of crazy going on in there.

13:37.740 --> 13:43.340
The first thing we saw was this idea that you had two distinct but complementary sides

13:43.340 --> 13:45.280
to your brand personality.

13:45.280 --> 13:48.180
The Morgan Spurlock brand is a mindful play brand.

13:48.180 --> 13:52.040
Those are juxtaposed very nicely together, and I think there's almost a paradox with

13:52.040 --> 13:53.040
those.

13:53.040 --> 13:57.740
I think some companies will just focus on one of their strengths or the other instead

13:57.740 --> 13:59.080
of focusing on both.

13:59.080 --> 14:04.920
Most companies tend to, it's human nature, to avoid things that they're not sure of,

14:04.920 --> 14:06.340
avoid fear, those elements.

14:06.340 --> 14:09.300
You really embrace those, and you actually turn them into positives for you.

14:09.300 --> 14:10.340
It's a neat thing to see.

14:10.340 --> 14:12.020
What other brands are like that?

14:12.020 --> 14:18.820
The first one here is a classic Apple, and you can see here, too, Target, We, Mini from

14:18.820 --> 14:20.700
Mini Coopers, and JetBlue.

14:20.700 --> 14:24.660
There's playful brands and mindful brands, those sort of things that have come and gone,

14:24.660 --> 14:28.400
but a playful, mindful brand is a pretty powerful thing.

14:28.400 --> 14:29.500
A playful, mindful brand.

14:29.500 --> 14:30.500
What is your brand?

14:30.500 --> 14:33.260
If somebody asked you to describe your brand identity, your brand personality, what would

14:33.260 --> 14:34.260
you be?

14:34.260 --> 14:35.900
Are you an up attribute?

14:35.900 --> 14:38.360
Are you something that gets the blood flowing?

14:38.360 --> 14:39.500
Are you more of a down attribute?

14:39.500 --> 14:43.260
Are you something that's a little more calm, reserved, conservative?

14:43.260 --> 14:49.420
Up attributes are things like being playful, being fresh, like the Fresh Prince, contemporary,

14:49.420 --> 14:55.860
adventurous, edgy or daring like Errol Flynn, nimble or agile, profane, domineering, magical

14:55.860 --> 14:58.080
or mystical like Gandalf.

14:58.080 --> 14:59.900
Are you more of a down attribute?

14:59.900 --> 15:02.100
Are you mindful, sophisticated like 007?

15:02.100 --> 15:07.220
Are you established, traditional, nurturing, protective, empathetic like the Oprah?

15:07.220 --> 15:13.340
Are you reliable, stable, familiar, safe, secure, sacred, contemplative or wise like

15:13.340 --> 15:16.340
the Dalai Lama or Yoda?

15:16.340 --> 15:22.420
Over the course of this film, we had 500 plus companies who were up and down companies say

15:22.420 --> 15:23.940
no, they didn't want any part of this project.

15:23.940 --> 15:27.120
They wanted nothing to do with this film, mainly because they had no control.

15:27.120 --> 15:31.220
They would have no control over the final product, but we did get 17 brand partners

15:31.220 --> 15:34.420
who were willing to relinquish that control, who wanted to be in business with someone

15:34.420 --> 15:40.420
as mindful and as playful as myself, and who ultimately empowered us to tell stories

15:40.420 --> 15:44.300
that normally we wouldn't be able to tell, stories that an advertiser would normally

15:44.300 --> 15:46.140
never get behind.

15:46.140 --> 15:50.580
They enabled us to tell the story about neuromarketing as we got into telling the story in this film

15:50.580 --> 15:54.820
about how now they're using MRIs to target the desire centers of your brain for both

15:54.820 --> 15:58.300
commercials as well as movie marketing.

15:58.300 --> 16:01.820
We went to San Paolo where they have banned outdoor advertising in the entire city.

16:01.820 --> 16:07.520
For the past five years, there's no billboards, there's no posters, there's no flyers, nothing.

16:07.520 --> 16:11.940
And we went to school districts where now companies are making their way into cash-strapped

16:11.940 --> 16:15.700
schools all across America.

16:15.700 --> 16:19.020
What's incredible for me is the projects that I've gotten the most feedback out of, or I've

16:19.020 --> 16:21.900
had the most success, and are ones where I've interacted with things directly.

16:21.900 --> 16:23.540
And that's what these brands did.

16:23.540 --> 16:24.700
They cut out the middleman.

16:24.700 --> 16:28.300
They cut out their agencies and said, maybe these agencies don't have my best interests

16:28.300 --> 16:29.300
in mind.

16:29.300 --> 16:31.260
I'm going to deal directly with the artist.

16:31.260 --> 16:34.500
I'm going to work with him to create something different, something that's going to get people

16:34.500 --> 16:37.220
thinking, that's going to challenge the way we look at the world.

16:37.220 --> 16:38.400
And how has that been for them?

16:38.400 --> 16:39.400
Has it been successful?

16:39.400 --> 16:42.740
Well, since the film premiered at the Sundance Film Festival, let's just take a look.

16:42.740 --> 16:46.660
According to Burrells, the movie premiered in January, and since then, and this isn't

16:46.660 --> 16:50.860
even the whole thing, we've had more than 900 million media impressions for this film.

16:50.860 --> 16:52.900
That's literally covering just like a two and a half week period.

16:52.900 --> 16:55.440
That's only online, no print, no TV.

16:55.440 --> 16:56.900
The film hasn't even been distributed yet.

16:56.900 --> 16:57.980
It's not even online.

16:57.980 --> 16:59.100
It's not even streaming.

16:59.100 --> 17:01.980
It's not even been out into other foreign countries yet.

17:01.980 --> 17:07.140
So ultimately, this film has already started to gain a lot of momentum, and not bad for

17:07.140 --> 17:12.680
a project that almost every ad agency we talk to advise their clients not to take part.

17:12.680 --> 17:16.620
What I always believe is that if you take chances, if you take risks, that in those

17:16.620 --> 17:18.600
risks will come opportunity.

17:18.600 --> 17:23.340
I believe that when you push people away from that, you're pushing them more towards failure.

17:23.340 --> 17:27.220
I believe that when you train your employees to be risk averse, then you're preparing your

17:27.220 --> 17:30.420
whole company to be reward challenged.

17:30.420 --> 17:34.100
I feel like that what has to happen moving forward is we need to encourage people to

17:34.100 --> 17:35.420
take risks.

17:35.420 --> 17:39.140
We need to encourage people to not be afraid of opportunities that may scare them.

17:39.140 --> 17:42.740
Ultimately, moving forward, I think we have to embrace fear.

17:42.740 --> 17:52.420
We've got to put that bear in a cage.

17:52.420 --> 17:53.420
Embrace fear.

17:53.420 --> 17:55.620
Embrace risk.

17:55.620 --> 18:01.580
One big spoonful at a time, we have to embrace risk, and ultimately, we have to embrace transparency.

18:01.580 --> 18:06.380
Today, more than ever, a little honesty is going to go a long way.

18:06.380 --> 18:11.660
And that being said, through honesty and transparency, my entire talk, Embrace Transparency, has

18:11.660 --> 18:19.740
been brought to you by my good friends at EMC, who for $7,100 bought the naming rights

18:19.740 --> 18:37.060
on eBay, EMC turning big data into big opportunity for organizations all over the world.

18:37.060 --> 18:40.100
EMC presents Embrace Transparency.

18:40.100 --> 18:41.100
Thank you very much, guys.

18:41.100 --> 18:42.100
Thank you.

18:42.100 --> 18:43.100
Thank you.

18:43.100 --> 18:44.100
Thank you.

18:44.100 --> 18:45.100
Thank you.

18:45.100 --> 18:46.100
Thank you.

18:46.100 --> 18:47.100
Thank you.

18:47.100 --> 18:48.100
Thank you.

18:48.100 --> 18:49.100
Thank you.

